WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
Gauntlet 2 for 1 to 4 players.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:26.920
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.100
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.640
And multiplayer really is the hook here, right?

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.240
There's a reason that the arcade game has four joysticks.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.340
There's a reason that they're highlighting on the back of the box that it's for 1 to 4 players.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.580
This can be a single player game, but it's a lot more fun the more people you have.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:53.060
I'm actually, you know, surprised that, you know, we've described that it's just whatever has the biggest text on the back of the box.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.620
I'm surprised that it was that.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:00.740
But I guess, you know, knowing what this game is, having played it, I guess I'm not too surprised.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:06.660
It's just like, you know, I'm used to the most fun you'll ever have with anything ever.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.680
And it's just very descriptive.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.180
Yes. Yeah.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.560
For 1 to 4 players feels like a spec, right?

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.560
Like on the features list.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.720
The bottom right. Yeah.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.520
But I guess what I mean is, is like, you can play this game single player.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.860
And I don't think like that would change our review of it, whether you had one or four players.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:33.140
But the game relies almost on a certain kind of chaos.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.000
Yeah. That, you know, more players are welcomed.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:47.540
It's similar to the beat-em-up games that you play in an arcade where like, you can play the Simpsons arcade game and just be Marge with a vacuum.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:54.460
And that's kind of wacky, but you could bring three of your friends and now you play as the whole Simpsons cast as you go through a level.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.940
And that's a lot more like fun, even though nothing about the gameplay has changed.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.340
You don't get to do like cool things together.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.140
If anything, you're kind of like competing for points in a weird way.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:11.800
Yeah. It's the same thing here with Gauntlet 2 where, and you know, Gauntlet 1 didn't have the four players.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.140
That was just two players.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:18.800
So to have four players here really brings home the arcade experience for real this time.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.660
Now, do you think that this was sold like with the attachment?

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.260
It did not.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.120
It was not sold with the...

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.840
Because what else had four players at this point?

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:39.680
Right. So there was the four score and there was the, I think like the, it was called the NES satellite, which is like a weird wireless way to also hook up for.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.280
Yeah. I'm like, I'm sure that that had incredible lag that nobody ever wanted to play.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.740
I mean, like they bought it because they thought it would be cool to have a wireless controller.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.980
I don't know about you, but I always wanted wireless controllers.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.700
Oh yeah, that WaveBird was cool for the, for the GameCube.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:03:01.400
Yeah. WaveBird was great because it actually worked, but you would buy like a wireless guitar hero guitar and you just wouldn't be able to play any of the songs because you'd be way out of rhythm.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.820
You know, there's a timing to these things.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.840
And this isn't the first four player game.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:16.620
Uh, it's certainly a great example of having four players, but I couldn't tell you what the first one actually was.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:23.880
But I vaguely remember like having it be an offhand comment in some previous episode.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.140
I just can't remember.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:32.340
Yeah. Like, uh, I, uh, Magic Johnson's fast break, I think was the one where I mentioned it last.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:33.580
Oh wow. Okay.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.420
Yeah. And I mean that game, that's the one where, you know, it kind of sucked.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:45.000
Yeah. But also like the vision of Magic Johnson, uh, comes to you in dream sequences between rounds.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.100
Oh yeah. The, the, the, the Magic Johnson apparition.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.100
Yeah. This is his game.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:04:03.280
I think we were calling it in the episode. But to be honest, when I'm looking at this list of four player games that we haven't even played yet, none of them seem like bigger wins than, uh, than this kind of arcade conversion.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:15.940
You know, uh, I really think if you played gauntlet to first in a bowling alley or at an arcade and in a mall or something, and then found out you could bring that whole experience actually home.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:33.600
I think that's the biggest sell for this kind of thing is similar to like what Sega would wind up doing with, uh, a lot of their, uh, Sega Saturn, but also dreamcast games where it was like, they were porting a lot of their arcade games, but also bringing with it the weird controllers too.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.760
So it was like, wow, I have this controller that only works on this one game.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.940
So I can finally replicate that weird experience I had in the arcade.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:44.020
And Gauntlet 2 is doing that to a bit of a lesser extent, but still entirely faithful.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:52.420
Uh, all the other ports of Gauntlet 2 for home computers and the like only supported the two players that the original NES game supported.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:57.620
So you're saying that like, uh, all the, so all those dreamcast games have the weird controllers.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.840
Those were, uh, arcade games.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.500
So you're saying that there's like a Seaman arcade game up there.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.940
I wish there was a Seaman arcade game that you got like, uh, yeah, you had like a ticket that you had to feed it.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.780
That had your fish on it, but not, not how it works.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.760
Like the horse racing ones. Yeah. Yes, absolutely.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:15.860
Like the horse racing ones.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.380
Uh, back onto this game for a little bit though.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:28.960
Yeah, yeah. Uh, the NES version of the first Gauntlet game was not a, uh, was not like a direct port of the arcade game.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.280
It had, uh, it had some more like, it had like a story and an ending to it.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.220
But Gauntlet 2 on the NES is pretty much just like losing.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.840
It doesn't even pretend. Right.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.160
Drops all the flare and just brings you a straight port.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:52.700
Do you think that, that, that is like subtractive that it's kind of like a knock on it that the, that we're just getting the arcade game and nothing extra.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:06:05.580
There is kind of this idea later on, uh, as arcades were starting to fade out that like, yeah, you would get the arcade game mode, but then you would also get like a story mode or something in a lot of those light gun games.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:16.900
Or, um, uh, crazy taxi was a good example of that where like in the arcade version, it's like, you just drive around the taxi, but then there was more like, not narrative, but like, yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.020
Kind of a, a progress system.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.880
Yeah. Gauntlet 2 is not offering that.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.620
I mean, let's, let's be honest.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:43.600
If we're just talking about the differences between gauntlet one and two and not a, um, and not some semblance of a, of a like career mode, which, which has a different progression or like a more robust progression is what you would say in maybe like a, a crazy taxi.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:52.800
It, because it really just is level, level after level, but instead, uh, there'd also be like, yeah, that some story.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.940
I don't think we're really missing much here.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.900
Like, do you remember the story of gauntlet?

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:07.160
That was it ever that conspicuous or was it just like a, uh, a pop-up that might show up and say some, some words?

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:08.860
No, you bring up a good point.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:12.900
Uh, I mean, we're limited in general on narratives in these games.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:14.580
Exactly. On the NES.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:20.000
Yeah. So, you know, gauntlet two is more like, um, adventures of low, low two.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:23.240
It's just an expanded version of the original game.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:28.040
Yeah. But I'm not, I'm not entirely that upset about it.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:33.500
I, it's actually kind of refreshing how pick up and play this game really is.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:42.180
Yeah. You really don't have to read a manual to understand anything about, I mean, like you, it will help you understand what the game is, is offering.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.560
It'll help you pick a character at the very least.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:50.080
Yeah, sure. But I guess, but to your point, like it is very pick up and play as an arcade game should be, right?

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:53.740
Like nobody's reading the side of the cabinet to figure out the rules, right?

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:54.880
True, true.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.300
I mean, sometimes.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:26.280
But there are like, there are some changes here too.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:30.760
You can't even have enemies spawn or bases where, uh, where those enemies spawn out of.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.060
Yeah, the little huts that come out of, yeah. Yeah, the huts, yeah, you gotta destroy those, you gotta destroy the enemies.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.580
Or you could do none of that.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.940
You could literally just be a pacifist and try to run through all this shit and just find the exit.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:46.020
But there's also, uh, keys you need to collect to open up gates to get through that stuff.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:54.960
Or, uh, warp points or just, you know, other things that kind of make it not puzzly, but in a way, uh, disoriented.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:07.020
Yeah. Yes. Um, the way that it works with like switches and keys and balancing like how many keys you have on you and, and how many, like, I don't know what those are.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:09.900
Like power potions on you.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:15.340
Um, cause you can only carry a certain amount, a total amount of those.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:25.880
Um, and, and just the way that those like, uh, like teleporters interact and, and it can be more puzzly.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:30.880
Than actioning in certain, in certain, uh, I guess boards you call them.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.780
Especially because the boards have some, uh, additional, uh, design elements.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:45.500
Uh, there are modifiers to these levels too, such as, you know, all the walls being invisible or, uh, stun tiles and stuff like that.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:52.520
Like the, the, the additional elements that were added into the game, uh, not only to like change up specific levels,

00:09:52.520 --> 00:10:04.380
but also to change a level that you might have encountered in a previous run of gauntlet two can be changed by just what they do is they, uh, they rotate the whole map.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:05.600
So like 90 degrees.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.520
So it's kind of familiar to you.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:16.180
Like it might've felt like you'd been there before, but for this playthrough, this time it's, you know, flipped horizontally or vertically in another way.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:22.240
So that, uh, it's, it's just a little bit different and you might not exactly remember exactly where the exit is.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:31.940
Yeah. I don't know how much I'd be able to notice that because of how, uh, you know, the, the, the, the colors change and the template changes,

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:36.300
but it's all kind of samey, but it's still a, an interesting concept.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:47.160
The most interesting concept in terms of new features to the game though, I think is that new enemy, uh, described as the it enemy,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:52.840
where, uh, if you get, uh, if you touch this enemy, you become it.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:57.500
And all the enemies are now drawn towards that specific player.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:05.960
And the only way to, uh, not be it anymore is by touching another player or exiting the, the map.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:12.500
So essentially it becomes like a game, a tag and you kind of turn against all the other players because you don't want to be it.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:18.180
So you actually have a reason to like run into and, and, uh, affect your other players.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:24.480
Yeah. It kind of feels more, more party mode than one would think from a game like this.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:31.740
And it's too bad that we can really only talk about these features like in concept because I only played by myself.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:33.760
I don't know if you recruited your wife.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:40.780
I couldn't, I couldn't get her to get on board with the top down video games.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:44.220
If it was, if it was an actual party game, like you were describing, we might have a shot.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:53.560
Yeah. But I, even just the single player version of being it is a, is a scary element, you know,

00:11:53.620 --> 00:12:01.020
like you don't want to come into contact with that because it's going to have the enemies drawn to you in a way that they otherwise in,

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.760
in the same stages, you know, they act more, they act a little more freely.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:07.240
Yeah. It's passive. Yeah.

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:21.620
And, and, and, uh, it's kind of, um, it, it, it's kind of interesting that like the, the pathing AI, it feels kind of like you can kite them, uh, most of the time.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:27.740
And if you don't want to deal with a group, you can just sort of, uh, figure out a way around certain walls.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:32.460
And, and, and if you're, you're patient enough, you can, you can keep them at bay.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:40.460
I guess I just never became it, uh, with what you're describing because they were always, I was always just kind of trying to block them off that way.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:41.760
And it usually worked.

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:48.700
You would know if you became it because it's one of the many instances of the very cool voice narration in this game.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:53.480
I, I think it tells you, you know, like red wizard is it, you know, and like.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:54.540
It takes a lot of time.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:13:01.520
It took a few like go arounds for me to even understand that voice because it's so, it's so choppy.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:02.660
But yeah, that is cool.

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:12.100
I, yeah, I do think that something, something about it at the time was cool and now it's more like novel, right?

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:22.340
Yeah. Uh, but, but I enjoy it and I enjoy these, uh, these old voices, uh, synthesizing sounds coming out of these games.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:33.260
Cause I do think it adds something to the arcadey experience of it to have an announcer in what is otherwise like kind of a, uh, you know, a dungeon crawling.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:38.220
The warrior now has refractive shot.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:39.340
Right, right.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:40.360
I like it.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:50.860
And I could imagine in an N64 version, it would be like an actual like baseball commentator style narration to it, you know, and it would feel kind of campy.

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:56.940
But here it, uh, it has an element of, um, of freeform jazz to it, if you will.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.200
Jazz. Interesting. Okay.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:00.780
I like that.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:01.980
Oh, what else?

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:02.840
It's okay.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:13.400
So there are things that we probably talked about in Gauntlet one that we should address here that, uh, you know, just remain, uh, like losing health as time elapses still sucks.

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:15.140
It's just not a great feature.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.180
Still sucks, but I think it's still, I think it's still an elegant way to do it.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:21.260
Um. I think you held that position in the first episode as well.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:25.760
I'm, I'm glad that I have some internal consistency cause I wasn't sure.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:37.040
Um, but I mean, you all, you have so much of it that it's not like you're going to run out on, on, in like the first five levels unless you're really taking damage.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:38.380
No, I agree.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:41.040
And it, it encourages you to race for the exit.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:48.000
I get it. I just feel like it, it, it makes it, especially in a game that is just a direct arcade port.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:55.860
It makes it feel more like something that was designed to eat quarters than to necessarily like enhance the gameplay.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:03.700
If they really wanted to, instead of like your health could have been something that was tied to enemies attacking you so that you have to manage that.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:09.060
And then there's just a timer for each specific level to like, you know, like, like Sonic the Hedgehog.

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:10.460
More Mario, yeah.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:14.760
No, but they have timers in them and it's like, okay, that's a lot of time, you know?

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:23.920
Like you, you'd have to really, uh, take your time with it to, you know, so, so you don't want to just stand still and fight these enemies and collect points everywhere.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:32.480
Like you do have an incentive to get to the exit, but I think tying the same thing that otherwise is also inflicted by damage to enemies just isn't a great feeling.

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:35.860
No, no, I, I get that.

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:41.340
Um, I just have less of a problem with it because the game is so simple otherwise.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:54.120
Um, I think the problem with like the, the moment to moment gameplay that I have the most with this game, and maybe it's the same position that I held in Gauntlet.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:58.520
It's just that like the whole, like, cause you have two modes of attacking.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.800
There's melee and there's, you have a projectile.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:10.460
And it really only feels like you're actually doing something if you're shooting that projectile because melee is just walking into enemies.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:16.000
And there's no special animation for when you're walking into an enemy and hitting them.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:22.540
And there's not really any feedback, like, audio feedback either.

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:25.660
Uh, they just sort of disappear if you're actually hitting them.

