00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:43.020
Couldn't have two Warriors, yeah. Right, you couldn't have two of them.

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:46.460
Now you can. So, all around, that's an improvement.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:50.760
Do they need to really, like, feel incredibly different?

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:53.280
I don't know, because I think that changes the way this game is.

00:18:53.380 --> 00:19:00.340
Like, at its heart, the gameplay is the same regardless of who you choose to be, right?

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:06.620
Yeah. The elf doesn't behave differently than the Warrior in a way where, like, you're solving levels differently.

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:10.840
Like, at the end of the day, you are just dragging your cursor to an exit.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:14.200
Your avatar maybe is better than a cursor.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:20.960
I think cursor is actually the more descriptive way to say it.

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:24.180
I guess video games is just cursers.

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:34.460
And since they want you to get to the exits and they encourage that, it would make sense that eventually they would just fuck with you when it came to the exits

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:43.060
and eventually introduce fake exits, opening and closing exits so that you have to be in them at the right time, moving exits.

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:49.380
All great features, in my opinion, for this kind of game that's supposed to be, you know, quick bursts of levels.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.900
It's fun to get messed around with in that kind of way.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:20:04.980
It would be worse if, you know, there were, like, more dead-end paths that you, like, had to then, like, re-navigate back through the path you just went through and find another one.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:06.360
And it's like, oh, that's a dead-end too.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:09.500
And there's only one area of this maze that leads to the exit.

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:10.600
Like, that sucks.

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:15.780
Moving around in the exit is, like, more of a, like, rush to it while you know where it is kind of thing.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:22.940
That's fun. I was actually surprised at how infrequent it was that I found myself in a dead-end.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:29.040
Because even if it looks like that, there's usually, like, a breakable wall or a teleporter.

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:32.600
And, yeah, you can get turned around pretty easily.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:35.920
But the maps aren't that complex.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:44.280
And I did have, like, a quick chuckle in the level that you're describing where it's just all exits.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:50.500
Like, all of the walls are exits and you just kind of have to walk through all the walls.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:55.480
It was, I do also, I like those little things.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:59.480
Playing with its own tropes.

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:09.720
And I don't know how much this matters for a game that really is just a pure gameplay experience kind of game.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:11.780
But it's a pretty dull-looking game.

00:21:12.520 --> 00:21:13.740
Yeah, it is.

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:17.400
I mean, the colors are vibrant where it matters.

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:23.500
But, like, it just, it's more like here's a ton of sprites on a screen.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:28.900
And it's up to the viewer to just kind of, like, quickly scan the area and discern what's what.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:33.280
And use that as a way to move through these locations.

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:36.180
It's really not a knock on the game for me.

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:38.240
I don't know what they need to do.

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:39.700
I know what you're describing.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:43.500
I think that you're just talking about, like, the simplicity of it.

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:55.760
And I think that the simplicity combined with just that there are this many, like, sprites coming at you is, that is just the aesthetic of the game.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:22:02.000
And I think it, it's still very, it's still a very unique aesthetic.

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:04.980
Like, there's a lot of games that I think we've played.

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:10.100
I think we've played this game, like, before records of just, like, here's a screenshot.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:11.280
Do you know what game this is from?

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.520
And there are so many that I could not tell you.

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:17.720
Because they all, there's a lot of games that look like a lot of games.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:20.240
But I could tell you what Gauntlet is.

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:21.740
Because Gauntlet looks like Gauntlet.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:26.480
Yeah. And on that note, Gauntlet is very well branded, in my opinion.

00:22:26.600 --> 00:22:28.220
I think they have a great title.

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:29.360
That's kind of rare.

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:31.580
You know, it's kind of rare to have, like, a great title.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:37.760
It does describe the experience, right, of the idiom of going through the Gauntlet and everything.

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:41.680
But also the idea of, like, who knows how many stages there are.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:42.880
I think it might be 50 or something.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.780
And you have to clear them all to win the game.

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:48.280
Like, it's all, it's all great.

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:49.340
It's kind of surprising.

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:58.160
Or maybe it's not surprising that this series that, like, started as this thing didn't, like, continue to evolve into, like, nowadays.

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:01.580
Like, a AAA RPG with this kind of name.

00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:05.380
Or at least during, like, the Xbox 360 era, maybe it would have happened.

00:23:07.100 --> 00:23:11.720
Instead, this does feel like a franchise that, like, you know because of the title.

00:23:12.080 --> 00:23:15.600
And, again, you know what it looks like because you've seen it in magazines.

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:20.860
But, like, I don't know if that's true for, like, the next generation of gamers.

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:25.280
Oh, no. I mean, I know that there was a Gauntlet for PS2.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:27.540
I did, I think I played that.

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:31.840
If it wasn't that, then it was a Gauntlet for PS1.

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:38.440
So, it's not, like, a lot of games that are just stuck in the 8 or 16-bit era.

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:39.980
Like, it did come out.

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:48.200
But, and that was one of the few instances where, like, I did have experience with the series that we were talking about.

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:54.120
But, yeah, I haven't heard anyone talk about Gauntlet aside from ourselves.

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:57.380
In the last, like, 10 years.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:06.880
Apparently, the Gauntlet game on PS2, Gauntlet Seven Sorrows, describes itself as a hack and slash.

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:08.740
Was it, like, a Devil May Cry style game?

00:24:08.740 --> 00:24:21.760
No, it's just, like, it's just a, it actually is a pretty good iteration of what we're playing here.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:25.220
It's just, it's 3D now, and there's more inputs.

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:30.500
So, there's a few more things, like, things that you can do.

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:33.440
And Melee is more Melee-like.

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:41.000
It's like a 3D, omnidirectional beat-em-up.

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:43.980
Oh, that sounds pretty cool.

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:46.900
Yeah. So, it's like a dungeon crawler, too?

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:49.120
Yeah, I guess you could call it that.

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:52.180
You know, like, with that, because you said 3D, so, like...

00:24:52.180 --> 00:24:58.820
It's not a dungeon crawler, because I feel like that's a very specific, like, wizardry kind of thing.

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:00.580
But, um...

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:03.060
Right, this is still very fast-paced, is what you're saying.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:10.100
It's still very fast-paced. Anyways, it's kind of like if Diablo was direct control and didn't have, and wasn't as complex.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:33.420
This next comment's gonna sound like a knock at the developers.

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:34.360
And maybe it is.

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:36.740
But I wrote it down, so I'm gonna say it out loud.

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:42.660
I think that this is a game that would massively benefit from procedural generation.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:46.580
Because I can't tell any curation in the level design.

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.700
Things just kind of feel like they are where they are.

00:25:49.120 --> 00:25:53.400
And maybe that's because they are also able to just be rotated at 90 degrees on a whim.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:55.240
Or flipped.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.740
And it's still considered a great level.

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:00.020
But, do you know what I mean?

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:01.360
It's just kind of like...

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:06.420
Yeah. Everything is thrown onto the screen, and then you are just tasked with dealing with it.

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:12.720
Not necessarily like, oh, that's really smart that they put the ghosts over here, and the gates over here, and the key's up there.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:14.060
Like, just whatever.

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:16.000
Just like, go get the key and open up the gate.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:26.660
I think a lot of these games that sort of bill themselves as being mazes with X, it's just always gonna end up like this.

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:28.320
Because a maze is...

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:35.320
It just doesn't have any spaces that would have any use otherwise.

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:36.700
They are literally just...

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:38.540
It exists to...

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:41.620
To be an obstacle in itself.

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:44.500
And I don't find too much interest in that.

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:48.160
But I guess I just accepted that that's what it was gonna be like.

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:49.680
There are some...

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:56.440
Like, the higher concept levels, I would say, are cleverly designed.

00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:59.440
But there are way more just like...

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:09.400
Just sort of throw whatever into the level editor and see what happens than there are those like really cool ones.

00:27:10.220 --> 00:27:14.400
Yeah. And it's not generated, but the game is randomized.

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:19.660
Did you know that, Sean? Like, level 7 isn't always level 7?

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:23.700
Yeah, so apparently the first 5 are locked in order.

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:26.040
But after that, you can have any order.

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:29.320
Yeah, I kind of noticed that a little bit.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:32.800
I didn't know that it was for the rest of the game.

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:39.400
So in level 1, you can go and find the exit to level 2.

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:43.420
But the exit to level 6 is actually more accessible.

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:52.080
And I guess that's just for people that don't want to go through the quote-unquote early game.

00:27:53.120 --> 00:28:00.060
So when I was trying the other characters that you can be, I would just go and find that.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:01.220
Skip to level 6.

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:04.260
I did notice that it wasn't always the same level 6.

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:15.580
Yeah, and keep in mind, not only is that great to keep the game fresh, but those same levels that can appear in any order can also appear in any order mirrored or flipped.

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:18.860
So there's a lot of variation to it.

00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:29.940
And so I think, again, if you and four buddies beat this game, there's still an incentive to play again with your four buddies because you can experience a totally different...

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:31.360
You're basically playing Gauntlet 3 now.

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:40.040
Yeah, if we're going on the super iterative version of a sequel, then yeah, definitely Gauntlet 3.

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:43.780
Right, right. It's like when you finish Super Mario Bros.

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:46.120
and then you get offered the next adventure.

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:47.780
Nobody considers that Super Mario Bros.

00:28:47.860 --> 00:28:49.240
2, but it kind of is.

00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:56.400
Actually, every different mission in XCOM is a new XCOM.

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:59.420
Because that is randomized.

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:13.180
That's funny. And since I was taking a shot at the developer, I might as well give you a little making of that was included on the Wikipedia page for this.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:16.740
So you might have read it. I found it very amusing, so I'm going to share it with the listeners.

00:29:18.040 --> 00:29:19.740
This is about the making of Gauntlet 2.

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:24.300
So after the release of the original Gauntlet in 86, keep in mind we're talking about the arcades,

00:29:25.100 --> 00:29:32.200
Atari held a contest where players could submit level designs or new ideas for a Gauntlet sequel.

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:36.760
The winner of the contest were announced in April of 1986.

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:39.900
So like literally a month later, they were like...

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:43.540
Wow, that's quick. Yeah, they were like, it's March of 86, give us your ideas.

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:44.680
April, here's the winners.

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:46.200
We're not really even reading these.

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:47.920
We're just taking anybody who submitted them, right?

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:56.040
Yeah. Apparently, some of the winning ideas or whatever were implemented based on those submissions.

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:58.680
I don't know if that's true. I don't have a way to find out.

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:00.080
There's no citation?

00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:04.680
There's no citation for the ideas being implemented.

00:30:04.780 --> 00:30:08.200
The winners were announced, but not necessarily their ideas.

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:11.800
So... You know, it's not like...

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:20.360
So the Wikipedia page is implying that the actual levels that were submitted became levels in Gauntlet 2.

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:23.100
Yes. Which would, again, just help with my understanding.

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:24.080
There's no confirmation of that.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:28.980
My understanding, again, that it could just be procedurally generated and it would be just the same.

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:31.840
Right? They're just playing Super Mario Maker, but with Gauntlet.

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:34.260
Yeah. That is interesting.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:37.100
Why isn't there a level editor in this game?

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:46.800
So during the release of Gauntlet 2, Atari held a second contest where players were tasked to find secret rooms in the game itself.

00:30:47.060 --> 00:30:50.000
Now, I don't know if you know this, but there are secret rooms in the game.

00:30:50.120 --> 00:30:55.460
Yes. You have to do a specific thing in the level in order for the room to appear, but that can happen.

00:30:55.860 --> 00:31:10.060
And after you, like, find the secret room or whatever and send it in, the players submitted these things to an entry form for, like, the competition for Atari.

00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:13.980
And the grand prize was a U.S.

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:16.140
savings bond of $5,000.

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:21.340
Like, was it matured?

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:24.320
Like, did you have to let it mature?

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:26.060
I think you had to let it mature.

00:31:26.260 --> 00:31:29.240
I don't think it was, like, ready.

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:31.020
I don't think they were holding on to a U.S.

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:33.760
bond that was like, yep, this is at value now.

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:34.700
So here's $5,000.

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:35.740
But $5,000?

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:38.600
That's crazy. I mean, that's a lot of money back then.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:41.960
Yeah. I mean, that's still nothing to sneeze at today.

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:43.240
Yeah. You know what I'm saying?

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:47.760
They're not offering anybody any money for, like, if you can find the skulls in Halo, right?

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:51.200
They're not being like, hey, first person to find all the skulls in Halo gets $5,000.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:55.120
They're actually charging you to tell you where the scrolls are.

00:31:55.520 --> 00:31:56.420
Right. The skulls.

00:31:56.840 --> 00:31:59.000
Yeah. The scrolls are for a different game.

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:07.980
The Elder Scrolls, yeah. And then if you didn't win or if you just didn't win in time, the first 500 people also received a T-shirt.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:09.540
It kind of sucks. Yeah.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.180
It's kind of a bit of a downgrade. You had your chance at $5,000.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:15.040
And all I get was this lousy T-shirt.

00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:22.820
So it's cool that you can play with four players and get the arcade experience at home.

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:24.340
We talked about that at the top and everything.

00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:31.480
But nowadays, you can play the arcade at home, like the arcade arcade, not the NES version of it.

